      <p class="eyebrow">Lecture 1 · Listening Guide</p>
      <h1>That Hideous Lecture</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">The Severed Head, Headship, and the Body</p>

      <section class="thesis">
        <p><span class="lbl">Expanded Thesis:</span> Hierarchy is not the disease our age takes it for; it is the shape love has to take among creatures of equal worth and unequal function, where honor runs up, provision runs down, and every superior owes more than he gets. It dies two ways — the head that abdicates and the head that usurps — and That Hideous Strength is a novel about both, from a college that will not govern itself to a severed head in a cellar that governs a body it has no need of. The cure is not the abolition of headship but the sight of the one Head who was obedient unto death and therefore highly exalted: whose authority is expenditure, not extraction, and who never leaves the body exposed.</p>
        <p><span class="lbl">Shortened Thesis:</span> severance is not liberation, whichever end does the cutting. Hierarchy dies two ways — abdication and usurpation — and the cure is the Head who bled for the body.</p>
      </section>

      <h2>Outline</h2>
      <div class="outline">
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">I.</span> Two heads Lewis met before he was seventeen <span class="min">(7 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Capron: robed, installed, reverend — and no head at all. A father's laziness, not malice.</li>
            <li>Kirkpatrick, the Great Knock: not nice; kind. → Aslan: not safe, but good.</li>
            <li>Word pair #1: nice ≠ kind.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">II.</span> The old grammar <span class="min">(10 min)</span> — Westminster on the Fifth Commandment</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Superiors, inferiors, equals; our several relations. Honor up, provision down, justice across.</li>
            <li>Duties of superiors — read aloud together. To love, pray for, bless, protect, provide, soul and body.</li>
            <li>Sins of superiors: careless exposing, or leaving them to wrong. (hold)</li>
            <li>Sins of equals: usurping pre-eminence. (hold) → Word pair #2: abdication vs. usurpation.</li>
            <li>Equality: Lewis, "I am a democrat because I believe in the Fall." Medicine, not food. Equal worth ≠ equal function; collapsing them always serves the strong. (The village "simple" man — handle directly.)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">III.</span> Four hierarchies in the novel <span class="min">(15 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Bracton — offices legitimate, heads asleep, deep state of committee-men; the wood sold by procedural fog. Abdication.</li>
            <li>N.I.C.E. — will to power in a lab coat; the head with no body. All demand, no provision. Usurpation. And they are nice, not kind → Absalom: chariots, then the kiss. Faithful are the wounds of a friend.</li>
            <li>Mark &amp; Jane — hierarchy mutually snubbed. He abdicates by absence; she withholds and calls it independence. Starving each other, politely.</li>
            <li>St. Anne's — Ransom. Authority under authority; the wound that won't heal; charwoman and skeptic given real work. The only happy room in the book.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">IV.</span> Why a head <span class="min">(7 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>1 Cor 12: many members, unequal function, the head cannot say to the feet, I have no need of you. Eph 4: from the head the body is nourished. Col 2: not holding the Head. Eph 5: the measure is expenditure, not command — he is the saviour of the body.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">V.</span> Abdication, but God <span class="min">(5 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Bragdon Wood and Jane are the same story: the enemy flatters the negligent steward to reach past him to the treasure. Both times the treasure walks to Ransom. The heads abdicated; Providence did not.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">VI.</span> Hard and soft elitism <span class="min">(5 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Hard: the vanguard creed, still fluent in tech. Soft: the Inner Ring, the common cold of the soul; no one decides to become a scoundrel. Mark already held the innermost ring — membership two — and could not care, because it was his. The stars bent down over it anyway.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pt">
          <h3><span class="rn">VII.</span> The Head who never left the body exposed <span class="min">(5 min)</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Edwards: Lion and Lamb; infinite highness, infinite condescension; more dust, or a bigger dunghill. Mark 10: not to be ministered unto. Phil 2: obedient unto death, wherefore highly exalted. The head who wears scars.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="lg-head">Listening Guide</h2>

      <h3>Two words to listen for</h3>
      <ul class="words">
        <li><strong>Abdication</strong> — a head that holds the office and refuses the work. Nobody seizes anything; the head simply stops governing, stops protecting, stops providing, and what was his to guard is left in the open. The catechism calls it careless exposing, or leaving them to wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>Usurpation</strong> — authority taken by those to whom it was never given, over those for whom they feel nothing. Command without communion; all demand and no provision. The catechism calls it usurping pre-eminence.</li>
        <li>A third word, running underneath both: <strong>nice is not kind.</strong> Kirkpatrick wounded like a friend. Absalom kissed like an enemy.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The four hierarchies of the story <span class="note">(mark them as we go)</span></h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="hier">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>The head</th><th>How it goes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bracton College</td><td>offices legitimate, holders asleep</td><td>abdication</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The Institute (N.I.C.E.)</td><td>a head with no body</td><td>usurpation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mark &amp; Jane</td><td>mutually snubbed</td><td>absence, and withholding called independence</td></tr>
            <tr><td>St. Anne's</td><td>Ransom, himself under authority</td><td>the downward duty intact</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Questions for discussion</h3>
      <ol class="qlist">
        <li>Define abdication. In your own words, not mine. Where have you watched it happen?</li>
        <li>Define usurpation. What made the Institute's people so pleasant, and why was that not an accident?</li>
        <li>How did Bracton lose the wood? Notice that nobody stole it. Trace the actual mechanism — who was flattered, what was never quite put to a vote, and what the college had forgotten it was keeping.</li>
        <li>How did Mark nearly lose Jane? He was never cruel to her. What was he doing instead? Why does the enemy court the negligent husband so generously?</li>
        <li>How would a modern person respond to the household at St. Anne's? A charwoman, a skeptic, a scholar, a bear — each given work fitted to what they actually are, under a head who obeys. What would our age call that arrangement, and what would our age be missing?</li>
        <li>Has God delivered you from someone else's neglect? Both treasures in the novel walk past the schemers to the true head on their own. The heads abdicated; Providence did not. Where has that been true of you?</li>
        <li>Reread the catechism below and discuss. Where are you a superior? Which of these duties have you quietly stopped performing? Which of these sins would the people under you name first?</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Westminster Larger Catechism on superiors, inferiors, and equals</h3>
      <blockquote class="cat">
        <p><span class="q">Who is meant by father and mother (Q. 124):</span> By father and mother, in the fifth commandment, are meant, not only natural parents, but all superiors in age and gifts; and especially such as, by God's ordinance, are over us in place of authority, whether in family, church, or commonwealth.</p>
        <p><span class="q">The general scope (Q. 126):</span> The general scope of the fifth commandment is the performance of those duties which we mutually owe in our several relations, as inferiors, superiors, or equals.</p>
        <p><span class="q">The duties of inferiors (Q. 127):</span> The honour which inferiors owe to their superiors is, all due reverence in heart, word, and behaviour; prayer and thanksgiving for them; imitation of their virtues and graces; willing obedience to their lawful commands and counsels… bearing with their infirmities, and covering them in love, that so they may be an honour to them and to their government.</p>
        <p><span class="q">The duties of superiors (Q. 129):</span> It is required of superiors, according to that power they receive from God, and that relation wherein they stand, to love, pray for, and bless their inferiors; to instruct, counsel, and admonish them… protecting, and providing for them all things necessary for soul and body: and by grave, wise, holy, and exemplary carriage, to procure glory to God, honour to themselves, and so to preserve that authority which God hath put upon them.</p>
        <p><span class="q">The sins of superiors (Q. 130):</span> The sins of superiors are, besides the neglect of the duties required of them, an inordinate seeking of themselves, their own glory, ease, profit, or pleasure; commanding things unlawful… careless exposing, or leaving them to wrong, temptation, and danger… or any way dishonouring themselves, or lessening their authority, by an unjust, indiscreet, rigorous, or remiss behaviour.</p>
        <p><span class="q">The duties and sins of equals (Q. 131-132):</span> The duties of equals are, to regard the dignity and worth of each other, in giving honour to go one before another; and to rejoice in each other's gifts and advancement, as their own. The sins of equals are… the undervaluing of the worth, envying the gifts, grieving at the advancement or prosperity one of another; and usurping pre-eminence one over another.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="closing">The head cannot say to the feet, I have no need of you. <span class="ref">— 1 Corinthians 12</span></p>

